17592186045417 true] [17592186422168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422160 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422162 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422163 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422164 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422165 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422168 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422168 1077 "selection" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422168 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422160 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422168 1079 #uuid "e83d217a-41be-555a-a16a-343371ec8880" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422169 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422158 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422169 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422159 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422169 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422161 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422169 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422166 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422169 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422167 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422169 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422169 1077 "selection" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422169 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422166 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422169 1079 #uuid "f19fca36-2b89-53ba-85fc-7765f2211e66" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422169 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422170 1069 "q-82451" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422170 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(P∧Q)⇒(P∧R).$" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422170 1072 #uuid "2bdcd5e4-a0e7-512a-97bb-f847c06388f4" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422170 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(P∧Q)⇒(P∧R)≡¬(¬(P∧Q))∨(P∧R).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(P∧Q))∨(P∧R)≡(P∧Q)∨(P∧R).\n$$\n\nFinally, by the distributive laws, we have\n\n$$\n(P∧Q)∨(P∧R)≡P∧(Q∨R).\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $P∧(Q∨R).$" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422168 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422171 1069 "q-182574" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422171 1071 "$\\,$ Either apologize, or she won't forgive you.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422171 1072 #uuid "c4482556-1279-5932-bd2e-cbe957830200" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422171 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Apologize.\n\n- $B:$ She won't forgive you.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't apologize.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't apologize, then she won't forgive you.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422172 1058 #edb/ref 17592186422174 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422172 1062 #uuid "12d4c7cd-cbe3-5cf6-98ac-5ef2b58306b0" 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422172 1063 #edb/ref 17592186378712 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422173 1058 #edb/ref 17592186422176 13194139534454 17592186045417 true] [17592186422173 1062 #uuid "992a12e5-4030-5d06-bfe4-99bff662d13d" 13194